Of course! A well-structured footer is essential for any website as it provides important navigation links and legal information. Here's a simple example of a footer layout you can use:

```html
<footer class="site-footer">
 <div class="footer-content">
 <div class="footer-nav">
 <ul class="footer-links">
 <li><a href="/about">About Us</a></li>
 <li><a href="/contact">Contact</a></li>
 <li><a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a></li>
 <li><a href="/terms-of-service">Terms of Service</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 <div class="footer-info">
 <p>&copy; 2024 MyWebsite. All rights reserved.</p>
 <p>1234 Example St, City, Country</p>
 <p>Email: info@mywebsite.com</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</footer>

<style>
 .site-footer {
 background-color: #f8f9fa;
 padding: 20px;
 text-align: center;
 border-top: 1px solid #e7e7e7;
 }
 .footer-content {
 display: flex;
 justify-content: space-between;
 flex-wrap: wrap;
 }
 .footer-nav ul {
 list-style-type: none;
 padding: 0;
 }
 .footer-links li {
 display: inline;
 margin: 0 10px;
 }
 .footer-links a {
 text-decoration: none;
 color: #007bff;
 }
 .footer-info p {
 margin: 5px 0;
 }
</style>
```

This footer includes navigation links to key pages like "About Us", "Contact", "Privacy Policy", and "Terms of Service". It also contains contact information and a copyright notice to establish the legal ownership of content on the website.

