 list-style: none;
 padding: 0;
 margin: 0;
 }
 .footer-links li {
 display: inline;
 margin: 0 10px;
 }
 .footer-links a {
 color: #fff;
 text-decoration: none;
 transition: color 0.3s;
 }
 .footer-links a:hover {
 color: #ddd;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

 <footer class="footer">
 <ul class="footer-links">
 <li><a href="mailto:contact@example.com">Email Us</a></li>
 <li><a href="https://maps.google.com?q=123+Main+St">Find Us</a></li>
 <li><a href="tel:+1234567890">Call Us</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>&copy; 2024 Your Company Name</p>
 </footer>

</body>
</html>
```

This example sets up a clean, simple footer with links for email, location, and phone contact, styled to fit within a standard website layout. Let me know if you need further customization!

Certainly! You can make the footer sticky by using CSS to ensure it remains at the bottom of the viewport, regardless of the page content length. Here's how you can modify the CSS:

```css
html, body {
 height: 100%;
 margin: 0;
}

body {
 display: flex;
 flex-direction: column;
}

.footer {
 margin-top: auto;
}
```

Add this CSS to your existing styles. The key here is using `flexbox` on the body to support the layout structure, and setting the `footer` to automatically take up the remaining space at the bottom. Let me know if this works for you!
